as small business owners catch a  collective breath from the pandemic top  of mind now is a potential recession  despite this and inflation many say they  are feeling confident and resilient we  find out why  cabbage and american express company now  out with its latest small business  recovery report after surveying more  than 500 small business owners across  the country in a wide variety of  industries chief marketing officer brett  sussman says 80 percent of them say they  could withstand a recession because of  what they learned during the pandemic  small business owners are confident  they're feeling resilient and what the  pandemic did is they forced them to  create a larger playbook than they had  before  and that playbook is bigger and better  sussman says the pandemic forced them to  reinvent their business go back to the  basics focusing on their brand and  incorporating new digital and social  media channels the small business  recovery report also revealed small  business owners raised their prices on  average 21 percent over the past year  and a half one in five say they are  going to have to raise prices again as  the year goes on inflation is a real  concern and it's coming from three areas  their suppliers are charging them more  the raw material costs are going up and  supply chains are snarled and they  expect that continue across the year his  tips for any small business owner  looking to survive a recession be  creative and innovative get online and  that really can expand you know who  you're offering your product to it may  move you from local to more regional or  nationwide  use digital and test into social media  platforms  he adds the following other tips  offering gift cards from your store  online to customers is a big boost and  if you're a small business owner who can  subdivide your space take in a second  tenant with a supplementary business to  reduce your rental and overhead costs
 

